
00:01:29.746 --> 00:01:41.912
For my presentation today, I'm going to talk about the Cajee Fields in the north -west of Ireland, one of the largest Neolithic sites in the world.

00:01:41.912 --> 00:01:49.257
I recently visited this site and observed the work that is currently being done by a team of archaeologists there.

00:01:49.282 --> 00:01:55.911
The site was first discovered in the 1930s by a local teacher, Patrick Caulfield.

00:01:55.911 --> 00:02:04.763
He noticed that when local people were digging in the bog, they were constantly hitting against what seemed to be rows of stones.

00:02:04.850 --> 00:02:15.142
He realised that these must be walls, and that they must be thousands of years old for them to predate the bog which subsequently grew over them.

00:02:15.142 --> 00:02:21.913
He wrote to the National Museum in Dublin to ask them to investigate, but no one took him seriously.

00:02:21.913 --> 00:02:32.585
It wasn't until forty years later when Patrick Caulfield's son, Seamus, who would become an archaeologist by then, began to explore further.

00:02:32.585 --> 00:02:46.280
He inserted iron probes into the bog to map the formation of the stones, a traditional method which local people had always used for finding fuel buried in the bog for thousands of years.

00:02:46.280 --> 00:02:56.396
Carbon dating later proved that the site was over five thousand years old and was the largest Neolithic site in Ireland.

00:02:56.396 --> 00:03:07.844
Thanks to the bog which covers the area, the remains of the settlement at kg fields, which is over 5,000 years old, are extremely well preserved.

00:03:07.844 --> 00:03:21.579
A bog is 90% water, its soil so saturated that when the grasses and heathers that grow on its surface die, they don't fully decay but accumulate in layers.

00:03:21.579 --> 00:03:29.256
Objects remain so well preserved in these conditions because of the acidity of the peat and the deficiency of oxygen.

00:03:29.256 --> 00:03:40.439
At least 175 days of rain a year are required for this to happen. This part of Ireland gets an average of 225 days.

00:03:45.608 --> 00:03:52.262
The Neolithic farmers at Keiji would have enjoyed several centuries of relative peace and stability.

00:03:52.262 --> 00:04:01.338
Neolithic farmers generally lived in larger communities than their predecessors, with a number of houses built around a community building.

00:04:01.338 --> 00:04:07.882
As they lived in permanent settlements, Neolithic farmers were able to build bigger houses.

00:04:07.882 --> 00:04:17.180
These weren't round, as people often assume, but rectangular, with a small hole in the roof that allowed smoke to escape.

00:04:17.180 --> 00:04:25.727
This is one of many innovations and indicates that the Neolithic farmers were the first people to cook indoors.

00:04:25.727 --> 00:04:30.648
Another new technology that Neolithic settlers brought to Ireland was pottery.

00:04:30.648 --> 00:04:36.374
Fragments of Neolithic pots have been found in Keiji and elsewhere in Ireland.

00:04:36.374 --> 00:04:48.549
The pots were used for many things, as well as for storing food. Pots were filled with a small amount of fat, and when this was set alight they served as lamps.

00:04:48.549 --> 00:04:55.309
It's thought that the Keiji fields were mainly used as paddocks for animals to graze in.

00:04:55.309 --> 00:05:03.193
Evidence from the Keiji fields suggests that each plot of land was of a suitable size to sustain an extended family.

00:05:03.193 --> 00:05:12.825
They may have used a system of rotational grazing in order to prevent overgrazing and to allow for plant recovery and regrowth.

00:05:12.825 --> 00:05:20.503
This must have been a year-round activity, as no structures have been found which would have been used to shelter animals in the winter.

00:05:20.503 --> 00:05:27.792
However, archaeologists believe that this way of life at Keiji ceased abruptly.

00:05:27.792 --> 00:05:29.461
Why was this?

00:05:29.461 --> 00:05:34.171
Well, several factors may have contributed to the changing circumstances.

00:05:34.171 --> 00:05:39.585
The soil would have become less productive and led to the abandonment of farming.

00:05:39.585 --> 00:05:47.471
The crop rotation system was partly responsible for this, as it would have been very intensive and was not sustainable.

00:05:47.471 --> 00:05:50.471
But there were also climatic pressures too.

00:05:50.471 --> 00:06:00.719
The farmers at Keiji would have enjoyed a relatively dry period, but this began to change, and the conditions became wetter as there was a lot more rain.

00:06:00.719 --> 00:06:10.279
It was these conditions that encouraged the bog to form over the area which survives today. So now I'd like to show you some...
